<content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title><para><span>Here is a table of values. The two quantities, \( x \) and \(y \), are related.</span></para>
<table>
  <tgroup cols="2"><thead>
    <row>
      <entry>\( x \)</entry>
      <entry>\( y \)</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>\(1\)</entry>
      <entry>\(0\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(3\)</entry>
      <entry>\(8\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(5\)</entry>
      <entry>\(24\)</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(7\)</entry>
      <entry>\(48\)</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</tgroup></table>
<para>What are some strategies you could use to find a relationship between \( x \) and \(y \)? Brainstorm as many
  ways as possible.</para>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples:</para>
    <list>
      <item>See if there is an operation that could be done to \( x \) that would produce \( y \).</item>
      <item>Compare how the \( x \)-values change and the \( y \)-values change, and see if there is a pattern.</item>
      <item>See if the numbers in one column follow a special pattern and if that pattern could be connected to the
        numbers in the other column.</item>
      <item>Plot the \( x \)- and \( y \)-values on a coordinate plane and see if the graph looks like a familiar
        relationship.</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise></section>
</content>
